# question: Which alarms coincide with other alarms that already convey equivalent clinical or technical information?
# rationale: Tests two independent notions of equivalence between coinciding
#   alarms: a shared diagnostic property, or a shared technical pathway
#   produced by distinct sensors.
#
# CORRECTED after a self-audit (see ../cq_discoveries.md's "Audit
# correction" entry, and the CQ3/CQ4/CQ5 corrections it triggered) -- the
# first version of this query only tested the "clinical" half of its own
# wording (two coinciding alarms reaching the same PhysiologicalProperty).
# It never tested "technical" equivalence at all. Added as a second UNION
# branch, reusing CQ2's own trace definition (same FunctionalUnit, Sensor,
# and Signal TYPE, via two independent SignalAnalysis chains) rather than
# inventing a separate notion of "technical equivalence" -- coinciding
# alarms sharing an identical sensing pathway, produced by two distinct
# sensors, are technically equivalent in exactly CQ2's sense, just tested
# pairwise here instead of grouped.
#
# The two branches are not always disjoint: two independently-sourced
# alarms sharing the same Metric TYPE are necessarily also clinically
# equivalent whenever that metric has an approximates bridge (the bridge
# is asserted per TYPE, not per instance) -- confirmed on the validated
# positive case below, which fires on both branches simultaneously. The
# technical branch only adds genuinely NEW coverage for a metric with no
# approximates bridge at all (currently only metric:Pulse -- see
# ../cq_discoveries.md) -- not currently exercised by any two-device case
# in this corpus, but the branch is structurally necessary regardless of
# whether today's corpus happens to trigger it standalone.
#
# reasoning: required (same graph-shape notes as CQ9: snap:forPatient sits
# outside GRAPH ?g; the FunctionalUnit/Sensor/Signal structural chain is
# accumulated BACKGROUND, matched unscoped; Metric identity is per-tick
# CONDITION, matched inside GRAPH ?g -- see CQ9's header for why).
#
# Validated against the fabricated positive/negative pair (DATA/
# CQ_evaluation/events_data.csv, patients cq10_pos/cq10_neg) and re-checked
# for regressions across all six fabricated patients: only cq10_pos
# matches (on both branches at once, as expected -- SpO2 laag/Lage SpO2
# share both the same property AND the same full sensing pathway); no
# other patient spuriously matches under the added branch.

PREFIX mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX snap: <https://w3id.org/mda/snapshot/>

SELECT ?patient ?kind ?matchValue (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?label; separator=" | ") AS ?alarms) WHERE {
  ?g snap:forPatient ?patient .
  GRAPH ?g { ?alarm mda:hasLabel ?label . }
  {
    GRAPH ?g {
      ?analysis1 mda:producesMetric/mda:approximates ?matchValue .
      ?analysis2 mda:producesMetric/mda:approximates ?matchValue .
      FILTER(?analysis1 != ?analysis2)
    }
    BIND("clinical" AS ?kind)
  } UNION {
    ?sensor1 mda:sensorProducesSignal ?signal1 ; a ?sensorType .
    ?fu1 mda:hasSensor ?sensor1 ; a ?fuType .
    ?signal1 mda:analyzedBy ?analysis1 ; a ?signalType .

    ?sensor2 mda:sensorProducesSignal ?signal2 ; a ?sensorType .
    ?fu2 mda:hasSensor ?sensor2 ; a ?fuType .
    ?signal2 mda:analyzedBy ?analysis2 ; a ?signalType .

    FILTER(?sensor1 != ?sensor2)

    GRAPH ?g {
      ?analysis1 mda:producesMetric/a ?matchValue .
      ?analysis2 mda:producesMetric/a ?matchValue .
    }
    BIND("technical" AS ?kind)
  }
}
GROUP BY ?patient ?kind ?matchValue
ORDER BY ?patient ?kind
